<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vittala Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vitthala Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vittala Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple at Hampi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, dedicated to Vitthala, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and built mainly in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, with additions in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a>. It is famous for its stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and its musical pillars, and is part of the Group of Monuments at Hampi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
